# @DISTRO_NAME@

@DISTRO_NAME@ is a Linux distribution: Ubuntu underneath, and on top the Omarchy desktop (Hyprland, a Quickshell bar and menus, Omarchy's themes and tools), ported from Arch Linux to Ubuntu's package manager. The first machine it is built for is a Lenovo Legion Pro 7 laptop with Intel and NVIDIA graphics, used with an Apple Studio Display.

## Built on Ubuntu

@DISTRO_NAME@ is Ubuntu @BASE_VERSION@ (`@BASE_SERIES@`), installed from Ubuntu's own package archive: the same kernel, systemd, Mesa, drivers and security updates, managed with apt. Both app stores are ready out of the box: Ubuntu's snapd (the Snap Store) and Flatpak with Flathub. Nothing is installed and then stripped out; the system is assembled from Ubuntu's archive one package at a time, and the few parts Ubuntu lacks (the desktop, its fonts, a handful of tools) come from @DISTRO_NAME@'s own apt repository at <@APT_URL@>. The installer is a live USB image running Calamares, a graphical installer from Ubuntu's archive (rebuilt with two fixes), and it installs onto an encrypted disk by default.

## Installing software: Recommends are off

apt on @DISTRO_NAME@ installs a package's required dependencies (`Depends`) but not the packages it only recommends (`Recommends`), unlike a stock Ubuntu. The setting is `APT::Install-Recommends "false";` in `/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/50@DISTRO_ID@-no-recommends`, installed by `@DISTRO_ID@-base`, and it keeps an application install from pulling in GNOME or KDE pieces as side effects; what @DISTRO_NAME@ itself needs from Ubuntu's Recommends is named in its own packages, among them the TPM tools, Ubuntu's updated hardware database, PipeWire's realtime priority, game controller access for Steam, iPhone and Android phone support, and printer discovery. An application can now and then lack an optional piece its packager expected, such as a plugin or a helper. To see what a package recommends, and to install it with its recommendations once:

```sh
apt-cache depends <package> | grep Recommends
sudo apt install --install-recommends <package>
```

To turn Recommends back on for good, add a file of your own that apt reads after @DISTRO_NAME@'s (apt reads `/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/` in alphanumeric order, so its value wins), rather than editing or deleting @DISTRO_NAME@'s file, a configuration file of `@DISTRO_ID@-base` that dpkg would ask about when a new version changes it:

```sh
echo 'APT::Install-Recommends "true";' | sudo tee /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/99recommends
```

## Secure Boot

@DISTRO_NAME@ boots with Secure Boot on or off and never asks the user to enroll a key. Both the live USB and the installed system boot through Ubuntu's own signed chain: shim (signed by Microsoft, so the firmware trusts it) loads Canonical's signed GRUB and kernel. Secure Boot is supported, never required: @DISTRO_NAME@ does not check or change the firmware setting.

## NVIDIA and AMD GPUs

The installer detects an NVIDIA GPU and installs NVIDIA's open driver from the newest branch that has Canonical's prebuilt, signed kernel modules. Because Canonical signed the modules, the driver loads under Secure Boot without a key of your own, and because the driver packages ship on the USB image, the installation needs no network. AMD GPUs need nothing installed: they run on the `amdgpu` driver in Ubuntu's signed kernel and on Mesa, both already in the system.

On laptops with hybrid graphics, where the built-in screen hangs off the Intel or AMD GPU and a discrete NVIDIA or AMD GPU sits beside it, the desktop renders on the display GPU and applications default to it, so the discrete GPU is left alone until something asks for it:

- the app menu starts a program on the discrete GPU when its desktop entry asks for one (Steam's does), or when its id is listed in `~/.config/@DISTRO_ID@/dgpu-apps` (a line `-<id>` keeps a program on the display GPU);
- `@DISTRO_ID@-launch-dgpu <command>` runs one program on the discrete GPU (in Steam: `@DISTRO_ID@-launch-dgpu %command%`): through NVIDIA's PRIME render offload on NVIDIA, through Mesa's `DRI_PRIME` on AMD;
- CUDA programs use an NVIDIA GPU directly, with no launcher.

The AMD path is tested against simulated machines only, not yet on AMD hardware.

## Apple Studio Display

The display's brightness has no standard control on Linux: the Studio Display takes brightness commands over its USB connection. @DISTRO_NAME@ packages `asdcontrol`, the program that sends them (Ubuntu's archive does not have it), and the desktop's brightness keys and controls use it for the display, as Omarchy does on Arch. Ubuntu's `bolt` handles Thunderbolt device authorization for the display's Thunderbolt connection.

## The Omarchy desktop

[Omarchy](https://github.com/omacom/omarchy) is a Linux distribution: Arch Linux's base (kernel, pacman, boot loader, snapshots) with Omarchy's own layer on top, shipped as two Arch packages (the commands, the Quickshell shell, the themes, and the settings, units and starter files a new user gets) plus a package list and setup scripts that tune the Arch base. @DISTRO_NAME@ takes only that layer and puts Ubuntu underneath instead of Arch. It carries a fork of Omarchy @OMARCHY_TAG@ (<@OMARCHY_FORK@>, branch `@OMARCHY_PORT_REF@`) in which pacman and the AUR are replaced by apt (and, for the few applications Ubuntu does not ship as regular packages, the Snap Store or Flathub), the scripts that configured Arch's base are dropped in favor of Ubuntu's own configuration, and the package list is translated to Ubuntu's names. The build turns that fork into Debian packages, renamed at build time: `@DISTRO_ID@-desktop` (the metapackage that pulls in Hyprland, the applications and the rest), `@DISTRO_ID@-shell`, `@DISTRO_ID@-themes`, `@DISTRO_ID@-tools` and `@DISTRO_ID@-config`. A sixth package from the same source, `@DISTRO_ID@-desktop-apps`, is optional and not installed by default: two everyday applications no part of the desktop needs (the Evince document viewer and GNOME Disks), installed with `sudo apt install @DISTRO_ID@-desktop-apps`. Hyprland comes from Ubuntu's archive; Quickshell, which Ubuntu does not carry, is built for @DISTRO_NAME@'s repository. Updates arrive through apt like the rest of the system.

